1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,113
For more than seventy years, the United States government has been

2
00:00:04,163 --> 00:00:05,481
asked the same question.

3
00:00:05,531 --> 00:00:08,332
Are we alone, and if we are not, what do you know?

4
00:00:08,382 --> 00:00:10,384
The answers have changed many times.

5
00:00:10,434 --> 00:00:12,608
A flying disc became a weather balloon.

6
00:00:12,658 --> 00:00:15,230
A weather balloon became a secret spy project.

7
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,252
Strange lights in the sky became classified aircraft.

8
00:00:18,302 --> 00:00:21,502
And in our own time, Navy pilots have filmed objects that

9
00:00:21,552 --> 00:00:23,725
the Pentagon itself calls unidentified.

10
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:26,519
This film is not about believing or disbelieving.

11
00:00:26,569 --> 00:00:28,115
It is about the paper trail.

12
00:00:28,165 --> 00:00:31,878
The memos, the reports, the panels of scientists, and the official

13
00:00:31,928 --> 00:00:36,268
statements, many of them once stamped secret and now open for anyone to read.

14
00:00:36,318 --> 00:00:39,005
We will start in the summer of 1947, and we will

15
00:00:39,055 --> 00:00:41,855
end with the most recent report from the Pentagon.

16
00:00:41,905 --> 00:00:45,048
Along the way, we will see where the government told the

17
00:00:45,098 --> 00:00:48,355
truth, where it did not, and where it simply did not know.

18
00:00:48,405 --> 00:00:50,350
This is the story of the UFO files.

19
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:55,063
On the twenty-fourth of June, 1947, a private pilot named Kenneth

20
00:00:55,113 --> 00:00:59,253
Arnold was flying his small plane near Mount Rainier, in Washington State.

21
00:00:59,303 --> 00:01:03,103
He was searching for a missing transport plane, hoping for a reward.

22
00:01:03,153 --> 00:01:05,764
Instead, he saw something he could not explain.

23
00:01:05,814 --> 00:01:09,671
Nine bright objects, flying in a line, very fast, near the mountains.

24
00:01:09,721 --> 00:01:12,954
Arnold later said the objects moved like a saucer would if

25
00:01:13,004 --> 00:01:14,540
you skipped it across water.

26
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:18,333
A newspaper wrote it up, and a new phrase was born: flying saucers.

27
00:01:18,383 --> 00:01:21,900
Within weeks, reports were coming in from all over the country.

28
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:26,203
Farmers, police officers, airline crews, ordinary families on their porches.

29
00:01:26,253 --> 00:01:28,524
Hundreds of sightings in a single summer.

30
00:01:28,574 --> 00:01:32,657
To understand why this mattered so much, you have to remember the moment.

31
00:01:32,707 --> 00:01:35,997
The Second World War had ended less than two years earlier.

32
00:01:36,047 --> 00:01:37,696
The atomic bomb was brand new.

33
00:01:37,746 --> 00:01:41,206
The Soviet Union had gone from ally to rival almost overnight.

34
00:01:41,256 --> 00:01:45,169
Both sides had captured German rocket scientists, and both were racing

35
00:01:45,219 --> 00:01:46,358
to build new weapons.

36
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:49,642
So when people saw strange things in the sky, the military

37
00:01:49,692 --> 00:01:52,473
did not ask whether they were from another planet.

38
00:01:52,523 --> 00:01:54,737
It asked a much more practical question.

39
00:01:54,787 --> 00:01:55,700
Are they Russian?

40
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:58,701
That question would shape everything that came after.

41
00:01:58,751 --> 00:02:02,664
For the next twenty years, the government's interest in flying saucers

42
00:02:02,714 --> 00:02:04,249
was not really about aliens.

43
00:02:04,299 --> 00:02:05,664
It was about air defense.

44
00:02:05,714 --> 00:02:09,005
If the skies over America were full of objects nobody could

45
00:02:09,055 --> 00:02:12,798
identify, then the radar network, the fighter jets, and the warning

46
00:02:12,848 --> 00:02:16,365
systems built to stop a Soviet attack were not doing their job.

47
00:02:16,415 --> 00:02:19,309
And then, in the middle of that summer, came a press

48
00:02:19,359 --> 00:02:22,083
release from a small army airfield in New Mexico.

49
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:26,951
On the eighth of July, 1947, the public information office at

50
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:31,760
Roswell Army Air Field put out a statement that made headlines around the world.

51
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:34,947
It said the base had come into possession of a flying

52
00:02:34,997 --> 00:02:37,111
disc, recovered from a ranch nearby.

53
00:02:37,161 --> 00:02:39,516
The story behind it was simple at first.

54
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:43,003
A rancher named William Brazel had found a field of debris

55
00:02:43,053 --> 00:02:44,385
on the land he managed.

56
00:02:44,435 --> 00:02:48,774
Sticks, foil, rubber, tape, and tough paper, scattered across the ground.

57
00:02:48,824 --> 00:02:51,840
He brought some of it to the local sheriff, and the

58
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:53,524
sheriff called the airfield.

59
00:02:53,574 --> 00:02:55,147
The press release went out.

60
00:02:55,197 --> 00:02:56,530
Newspapers ran with it.

61
00:02:56,580 --> 00:03:00,197
For one day, it seemed the Army had captured a flying saucer.

62
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:02,421
The very next day, the story changed.

63
00:03:02,471 --> 00:03:06,389
At a higher headquarters in Fort Worth, Texas, General Roger Ramey

64
00:03:06,439 --> 00:03:08,073
showed reporters the debris.

65
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:12,281
He said it was the remains of a weather balloon and a radar reflector.

66
00:03:12,331 --> 00:03:13,664
Photographs were taken.

67
00:03:13,714 --> 00:03:17,511
The newspapers printed the correction, and the story faded away.

68
00:03:17,561 --> 00:03:20,397
For almost thirty years, Roswell was a footnote.

69
00:03:20,447 --> 00:03:23,884
Then, in the late 1970s, a retired officer who had handled

70
00:03:23,934 --> 00:03:28,093
the debris, Major Jesse Marcel, told interviewers that he believed the

71
00:03:28,143 --> 00:03:30,076
material was not from this world.

72
00:03:30,126 --> 00:03:30,978
Books followed.

73
00:03:31,028 --> 00:03:34,585
Witnesses came forward, some with stories of strange bodies.

74
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:38,613
By the 1990s, Roswell had become the most famous UFO case on earth.

75
00:03:38,663 --> 00:03:41,980
We will come back to Roswell later in this film, because

76
00:03:42,030 --> 00:03:44,866
the government did eventually give a new answer.

77
00:03:44,916 --> 00:03:47,090
But for now, keep one detail in mind.

78
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,457
In 1947, the official explanation was a weather balloon.

79
00:03:50,507 --> 00:03:53,583
As we will see, that was not the whole truth either.

80
00:03:54,667 --> 00:03:58,462
Six months after Roswell, the flying saucer story turned deadly.

81
00:03:58,512 --> 00:04:02,128
On the afternoon of the seventh of January, 1948, the control

82
00:04:02,178 --> 00:04:05,853
tower at Godman Field, next to Fort Knox in Kentucky, received

83
00:04:05,903 --> 00:04:08,077
reports of a large object in the sky.

84
00:04:08,127 --> 00:04:12,523
Officers in the tower could see it too: something bright, high above them.

85
00:04:12,573 --> 00:04:16,429
A flight of four F-51 Mustang fighters, returning from a training

86
00:04:16,479 --> 00:04:18,472
mission, was asked to take a look.

87
00:04:18,522 --> 00:04:22,678
The flight leader was Captain Thomas Mantell, an experienced pilot who

88
00:04:22,728 --> 00:04:24,721
had flown in the Second World War.

89
00:04:24,771 --> 00:04:29,288
Over the radio, he described the object as metallic, and tremendous in size.

90
00:04:29,338 --> 00:04:30,970
He began to climb toward it.

91
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:34,215
The Mustangs were not equipped for very high altitude.

92
00:04:34,265 --> 00:04:35,657
His wingmen turned back.

93
00:04:35,707 --> 00:04:40,104
Mantell kept climbing, to around twenty-two thousand feet, without oxygen.

94
00:04:40,154 --> 00:04:41,726
Then the radio went silent.

95
00:04:41,776 --> 00:04:45,692
His plane came down on a farm in Kentucky, and Mantell was killed.

96
00:04:45,742 --> 00:04:49,298
He was the first American pilot whose death became linked to

97
00:04:49,348 --> 00:04:52,062
a UFO, and the newspapers made the most of it.

98
00:04:52,112 --> 00:04:55,847
The first official explanation was that he had been chasing the

99
00:04:55,897 --> 00:04:59,092
planet Venus, which can sometimes be seen in daylight.

100
00:04:59,142 --> 00:05:01,075
Few people found that convincing.

101
00:05:01,125 --> 00:05:04,680
Years later, Edward Ruppelt, who by then was running the Air

102
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:07,865
Force's UFO investigations, looked at the case again.

103
00:05:07,915 --> 00:05:11,651
He concluded that the object was most likely a Skyhook balloon,

104
00:05:11,701 --> 00:05:16,698
a huge high-altitude research balloon used in a classified Navy program at the time.

105
00:05:16,748 --> 00:05:20,724
A balloon like that, catching the sunlight, could look exactly like

106
00:05:20,774 --> 00:05:22,827
a giant metallic object, far above.

107
00:05:22,877 --> 00:05:25,651
It was the first time, but not the last, that a

108
00:05:25,701 --> 00:05:29,317
secret government project would sit behind a famous UFO case.

109
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:33,294
By the end of 1947, the Air Force had decided it

110
00:05:33,344 --> 00:05:35,624
needed to study the reports seriously.

111
00:05:35,674 --> 00:05:39,917
In early 1948, it launched an investigation called Project Sign, based

112
00:05:39,967 --> 00:05:43,965
at Wright Field in Ohio, the home of its air intelligence experts.

113
00:05:44,015 --> 00:05:47,767
The investigators of Project Sign looked at hundreds of cases.

114
00:05:47,817 --> 00:05:52,366
Many were easy to explain: planets, balloons, aircraft, birds, reflections.

115
00:05:52,416 --> 00:05:53,470
But some were not.

116
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:57,763
According to later accounts, some members of the project went further.

117
00:05:57,813 --> 00:06:01,504
Edward Ruppelt, an Air Force officer who would later lead the

118
00:06:01,554 --> 00:06:05,919
government's UFO investigations, wrote that the Sign team prepared a top

119
00:06:05,969 --> 00:06:09,108
secret document called an Estimate of the Situation.

120
00:06:09,158 --> 00:06:12,543
In his account, it argued that some of the objects might

121
00:06:12,593 --> 00:06:13,953
be from another planet.

122
00:06:14,003 --> 00:06:17,694
Ruppelt wrote that the document went up the chain of command,

123
00:06:17,744 --> 00:06:20,147
was rejected, and was ordered destroyed.

124
00:06:20,197 --> 00:06:24,869
No copy has ever been found, so historians still debate exactly what it said.

125
00:06:24,919 --> 00:06:27,200
What we do know is what happened next.

126
00:06:27,250 --> 00:06:31,677
In 1949, Project Sign was replaced by a new effort called Project Grudge.

127
00:06:31,727 --> 00:06:32,903
The name says a lot.

128
00:06:32,953 --> 00:06:35,234
Grudge took a very different approach.

129
00:06:35,284 --> 00:06:39,036
Its goal was largely to explain sightings away and to reassure

130
00:06:39,086 --> 00:06:42,041
the public that there was nothing to worry about.

131
00:06:42,091 --> 00:06:43,451
For a while, it worked.

132
00:06:43,501 --> 00:06:44,433
Reports dropped.

133
00:06:44,483 --> 00:06:47,621
Then, at the start of the 1950s, a new wave arrived,

134
00:06:47,671 --> 00:06:50,565
and the Air Force needed something more serious.

135
00:06:50,615 --> 00:06:54,183
In 1952, it created the project that would last the longest

136
00:06:54,233 --> 00:06:56,269
and become the most famous of all.

137
00:06:56,319 --> 00:06:58,231
It was called Project Blue Book.

138
00:06:58,281 --> 00:07:00,561
Edward Ruppelt was its first director.

139
00:07:00,611 --> 00:07:05,100
He is also usually credited with popularizing the term unidentified flying

140
00:07:05,150 --> 00:07:08,105
object, a more neutral phrase than flying saucer.

141
00:07:08,155 --> 00:07:10,496
A U F O did not have to be a spaceship.

142
00:07:10,546 --> 00:07:14,421
It simply meant that, at least for now, nobody knew what it was.

143
00:07:14,471 --> 00:07:18,162
And that summer, nobody knew what was flying over the capital

144
00:07:18,212 --> 00:07:19,450
of the United States.

145
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:24,227
While the Air Force was running its projects, another agency was

146
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:28,240
quietly collecting UFO stories: the Federal Bureau of Investigation.

147
00:07:28,290 --> 00:07:31,309
The FBI's files on the subject are now public, in an

148
00:07:31,359 --> 00:07:34,083
online reading room the bureau calls The Vault.

149
00:07:34,133 --> 00:07:37,388
And one single page from those files has become the most

150
00:07:37,438 --> 00:07:39,807
viewed document in the entire collection.

151
00:07:39,857 --> 00:07:43,289
It is a short memo, dated the twenty-second of March, 1950.

152
00:07:43,339 --> 00:07:46,299
It was written by Guy Hottel, the head of the FBI's

153
00:07:46,349 --> 00:07:49,250
office in Washington, and addressed to Director J.

154
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:50,017
Edgar Hoover.

155
00:07:50,067 --> 00:07:53,263
It passes on a story that Hottel had heard from someone

156
00:07:53,313 --> 00:07:56,922
else, who said he had heard it from an Air Force investigator.

157
00:07:56,972 --> 00:08:02,175
According to that story, three so-called flying saucers had been recovered in New Mexico.

158
00:08:02,225 --> 00:08:05,657
Each was described as about fifty feet across, and each was

159
00:08:05,707 --> 00:08:09,257
said to contain bodies of human shape, about three feet tall.

160
00:08:09,307 --> 00:08:12,502
When the memo was released, many people saw it as proof

161
00:08:12,552 --> 00:08:14,037
of a crash and a cover-up.

162
00:08:14,087 --> 00:08:16,457
In 2013, the FBI addressed this directly.

163
00:08:16,507 --> 00:08:19,584
It said the memo did not prove the existence of UFOs.

164
00:08:19,634 --> 00:08:24,011
It was a second or third hand claim that the bureau had never investigated.

165
00:08:24,061 --> 00:08:27,375
And it said there was no reason to connect it to Roswell.

166
00:08:27,425 --> 00:08:31,388
Researchers have since pointed out that stories very similar to this

167
00:08:31,438 --> 00:08:34,634
one were going around in 1950, linked to a popular hoax

168
00:08:34,684 --> 00:08:37,289
about crashed saucers that was later exposed.

169
00:08:37,339 --> 00:08:40,240
But the memo itself is real, and it shows how fast

170
00:08:40,290 --> 00:08:43,250
these stories traveled, even inside the government.

171
00:08:44,333 --> 00:08:48,001
To understand why the government cared so much about objects in

172
00:08:48,051 --> 00:08:51,364
the sky in 1952, it helps to look at who was watching it.

173
00:08:51,414 --> 00:08:54,905
In the early 1950s, American radar could not see everything.

174
00:08:54,955 --> 00:08:58,917
Low-flying aircraft could slip under the radar beams, and there were

175
00:08:58,967 --> 00:09:01,042
gaps in coverage across the country.

176
00:09:01,092 --> 00:09:03,697
So the Air Force turned to ordinary citizens.

177
00:09:03,747 --> 00:09:07,474
It revived a volunteer program called the Ground Observer Corps.

178
00:09:07,524 --> 00:09:12,017
Volunteers, many of them retirees, farmers, and high school students, staffed

179
00:09:12,067 --> 00:09:15,558
small observation posts on rooftops, water towers and hills.

180
00:09:15,608 --> 00:09:19,216
They were trained to spot and identify aircraft by their shape

181
00:09:19,266 --> 00:09:22,698
and sound, and to phone in reports to filter centers, where

182
00:09:22,748 --> 00:09:27,418
the information was plotted on large maps and passed to air defense controllers.

183
00:09:27,468 --> 00:09:30,605
In July 1952, the same month that radar screens lit up

184
00:09:30,655 --> 00:09:34,972
over Washington, the Air Force launched Operation Skywatch, asking many of

185
00:09:35,022 --> 00:09:37,509
these posts to keep watch around the clock.

186
00:09:37,559 --> 00:09:40,872
Tens of thousands of eyes were now turned toward the sky,

187
00:09:40,922 --> 00:09:42,584
looking for anything unusual.

188
00:09:42,634 --> 00:09:44,000
You can see the problem.

189
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:47,422
A system built to catch Soviet bombers was also certain to

190
00:09:47,472 --> 00:09:51,789
catch balloons, meteors, bright planets, and strange lights of every kind.

191
00:09:51,839 --> 00:09:54,444
Every one of those reports had to be checked.

192
00:09:54,494 --> 00:09:58,280
The more people watched the sky, the more UFO reports there were.

193
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:01,643
And the more UFO reports there were, the harder it became

194
00:10:01,693 --> 00:10:04,299
to separate a real threat from a false alarm.

195
00:10:04,349 --> 00:10:07,367
And in July 1952, that system was about to be tested

196
00:10:07,417 --> 00:10:08,783
over the capital itself.

197
00:10:09,833 --> 00:10:13,256
On the night of the nineteenth of July, 1952, radar operators

198
00:10:13,306 --> 00:10:17,127
at Washington National Airport saw something they could not explain.

199
00:10:17,177 --> 00:10:21,225
Several unknown targets appeared on their screens, moving over the city.

200
00:10:21,275 --> 00:10:25,324
Radar at nearby Andrews Air Force Base reportedly picked up targets too.

201
00:10:25,374 --> 00:10:28,341
Some airline pilots in the area said they saw lights.

202
00:10:28,391 --> 00:10:30,276
Fighter jets were sent up to look.

203
00:10:30,326 --> 00:10:33,008
By the time they arrived, the targets were gone.

204
00:10:33,058 --> 00:10:35,399
When the jets left, the targets came back.

205
00:10:35,449 --> 00:10:39,839
One week later, on the weekend of the twenty-sixth of July, it happened again.

206
00:10:39,889 --> 00:10:42,116
More targets, more jets, more confusion.

207
00:10:42,166 --> 00:10:44,165
This time the newspapers were ready.

208
00:10:44,215 --> 00:10:48,833
Headlines across the country said that flying saucers had swarmed over Washington.

209
00:10:48,883 --> 00:10:51,281
The pressure on the Air Force was enormous.

210
00:10:51,331 --> 00:10:54,924
On the twenty-ninth of July, Major General John Samford, the Air

211
00:10:54,974 --> 00:10:59,080
Force director of intelligence, held one of the largest press conferences

212
00:10:59,130 --> 00:11:01,869
the Pentagon had seen since the Second World War.

213
00:11:01,919 --> 00:11:03,576
His explanation was technical.

214
00:11:03,626 --> 00:11:06,650
On hot summer nights, layers of warm air can sit above

215
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:08,187
cooler air near the ground.

216
00:11:08,237 --> 00:11:10,407
This is called a temperature inversion.

217
00:11:10,457 --> 00:11:13,538
It can bend radar beams and create false targets on the

218
00:11:13,588 --> 00:11:17,181
screen, and it can make distant lights appear in strange places.

219
00:11:17,231 --> 00:11:19,686
Samford said that was the most likely cause.

220
00:11:19,736 --> 00:11:21,394
Many people did not accept it.

221
00:11:21,444 --> 00:11:26,232
But inside the government, the Washington sightings caused a different kind of alarm.

222
00:11:26,282 --> 00:11:28,851
The concern was not that aliens were visiting.

223
00:11:28,901 --> 00:11:31,868
The concern was that a flood of UFO reports could jam

224
00:11:31,918 --> 00:11:35,511
the military's communication lines at the worst possible moment.

225
00:11:35,561 --> 00:11:38,869
If the Soviets ever launched a real attack, would anyone be

226
00:11:38,919 --> 00:11:41,659
able to tell real bombers from imaginary saucers?

227
00:11:41,709 --> 00:11:46,383
That question brought a new player into the story: the Central Intelligence Agency.

228
00:11:47,467 --> 00:11:51,768
In late 1952, officials at the CIA's Office of Scientific Intelligence

229
00:11:51,818 --> 00:11:53,882
decided they needed expert advice.

230
00:11:53,932 --> 00:11:58,855
So in January 1953, the agency brought together a small group of top scientists.

231
00:11:58,905 --> 00:12:02,461
It was led by Howard Percy Robertson, a physicist from the

232
00:12:02,511 --> 00:12:04,637
California Institute of Technology.

233
00:12:04,687 --> 00:12:08,864
Other members included Luis Alvarez, a physicist who would later win

234
00:12:08,914 --> 00:12:12,843
the Nobel Prize, and Samuel Goudsmit, a physicist famous for his

235
00:12:12,893 --> 00:12:14,272
work on atomic science.

236
00:12:14,322 --> 00:12:18,313
For four days, from the fourteenth to the seventeenth of January,

237
00:12:18,363 --> 00:12:21,732
the panel reviewed the best material the Air Force had.

238
00:12:21,782 --> 00:12:25,773
Case files, statistics, and two short films of moving lights, one

239
00:12:25,823 --> 00:12:28,757
shot in Montana in 1950 and one in Utah in 1952.

240
00:12:28,807 --> 00:12:33,606
The scientists heard from Air Force investigators and discussed what they saw.

241
00:12:33,656 --> 00:12:35,285
Their conclusion was clear.

242
00:12:35,335 --> 00:12:40,382
They found no evidence that the objects were a direct threat to national security.

243
00:12:40,432 --> 00:12:44,609
And they found no evidence that any of them came from another world.

244
00:12:44,659 --> 00:12:49,023
Most reports, they believed, could be explained by ordinary things that

245
00:12:49,073 --> 00:12:50,950
people were not used to seeing.

246
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,810
But the panel did see a danger, and it was the

247
00:12:53,860 --> 00:12:56,856
same one that worried officials after Washington.

248
00:12:56,906 --> 00:13:01,208
A constant flood of reports could clog military channels, cause panic,

249
00:13:01,258 --> 00:13:03,757
and make it harder to spot a real attack.

250
00:13:03,807 --> 00:13:08,481
So the panel made a recommendation that would cause controversy for decades.

251
00:13:08,531 --> 00:13:12,584
It suggested an education program to take the mystery out of UFOs.

252
00:13:12,634 --> 00:13:17,309
This could use mass media, including television and films, and even schools.

253
00:13:17,359 --> 00:13:20,914
The report mentioned Walt Disney as an example of the kind

254
00:13:20,964 --> 00:13:23,339
of popular communicator who might help.

255
00:13:23,389 --> 00:13:27,255
It also suggested keeping an eye on private UFO groups, because

256
00:13:27,305 --> 00:13:30,115
of their possible influence on public opinion.

257
00:13:30,165 --> 00:13:32,788
For years, the full report was kept secret.

258
00:13:32,838 --> 00:13:36,394
When it finally became public, many people saw it as proof

259
00:13:36,444 --> 00:13:41,118
that the government had decided to debunk UFOs rather than investigate them.

260
00:13:41,168 --> 00:13:46,278
Others saw it as a sober scientific review that simply found nothing extraordinary.

261
00:13:46,328 --> 00:13:49,076
Both readings can be found in the same pages.

262
00:13:49,126 --> 00:13:52,744
What is certain is that after 1953, the official message to

263
00:13:52,794 --> 00:13:55,417
the public became more and more dismissive.

264
00:13:56,467 --> 00:13:59,708
For the rest of the 1950s and through the 1960s, Project

265
00:13:59,758 --> 00:14:03,175
Blue Book was the public face of the government's UFO work.

266
00:14:03,225 --> 00:14:06,701
It was based at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio, and

267
00:14:06,751 --> 00:14:09,464
it collected reports from all over the country.

268
00:14:09,514 --> 00:14:11,109
The project was never large.

269
00:14:11,159 --> 00:14:14,165
For most of its life it had only a handful of staff.

270
00:14:14,215 --> 00:14:17,809
Its job was to receive reports, investigate the ones it could,

271
00:14:17,859 --> 00:14:20,512
and give each case an explanation if possible.

272
00:14:20,562 --> 00:14:24,097
One of its most important advisers was an astronomer named J.

273
00:14:24,147 --> 00:14:24,803
Allen Hynek.

274
00:14:24,853 --> 00:14:26,566
At first, Hynek was a skeptic.

275
00:14:26,616 --> 00:14:30,033
He believed that almost every sighting would turn out to be

276
00:14:30,083 --> 00:14:32,560
a planet, a star, a balloon or an aircraft.

277
00:14:32,610 --> 00:14:35,440
But over twenty years of work, his views changed.

278
00:14:35,490 --> 00:14:38,731
He came to believe that a small number of cases deserved

279
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:42,551
much more serious scientific study than they were getting, and he

280
00:14:42,601 --> 00:14:45,490
later criticized the way the project had been run.

281
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:47,899
The low point for Blue Book came in 1966.

282
00:14:47,949 --> 00:14:51,425
In March of that year, dozens of people in Michigan reported

283
00:14:51,475 --> 00:14:53,659
strange lights near swamps and fields.

284
00:14:53,709 --> 00:14:55,422
Hynek was sent to investigate.

285
00:14:55,472 --> 00:14:58,889
At a press conference, he suggested that some of the lights

286
00:14:58,939 --> 00:15:03,179
might have been caused by marsh gas, glowing naturally over the wetlands.

287
00:15:03,229 --> 00:15:05,001
The public reaction was fierce.

288
00:15:05,051 --> 00:15:08,351
The phrase swamp gas became a national joke, and a symbol

289
00:15:08,401 --> 00:15:11,172
of an official explanation that nobody believed.

290
00:15:11,222 --> 00:15:15,580
A congressman from Michigan, Gerald Ford, who would later become President,

291
00:15:15,630 --> 00:15:18,518
called for a congressional hearing on the subject.

292
00:15:18,568 --> 00:15:23,161
In April 1966, the House Armed Services Committee held a short hearing on UFOs.

293
00:15:23,211 --> 00:15:24,689
The result was a new idea.

294
00:15:24,739 --> 00:15:27,922
Instead of letting the Air Force grade its own work, an

295
00:15:27,972 --> 00:15:31,272
outside university would study the problem independently.

296
00:15:31,322 --> 00:15:34,092
That study would decide the future of Blue Book.

297
00:15:34,142 --> 00:15:37,207
But before we get there, we need to look at something

298
00:15:37,257 --> 00:15:39,676
the public was not told about at the time.

299
00:15:39,726 --> 00:15:43,026
Because some of the strangest objects in the sky in those

300
00:15:43,076 --> 00:15:46,317
years were real, and they belonged to the United States.

301
00:15:47,433 --> 00:15:50,472
In 1955, the CIA began testing a new aircraft at a

302
00:15:50,522 --> 00:15:52,510
remote base in the Nevada desert.

303
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:55,599
It was the U-2, a spy plane designed to fly higher

304
00:15:55,649 --> 00:15:58,008
than any plane the Soviets could reach.

305
00:15:58,058 --> 00:16:02,764
At the time, commercial airliners flew at around ten to twenty thousand feet.

306
00:16:02,814 --> 00:16:05,297
The U-2 flew at more than sixty thousand.

307
00:16:05,347 --> 00:16:07,892
At that height, something unusual happens.

308
00:16:07,942 --> 00:16:10,609
As the sun sets, the ground below goes dark.

309
00:16:10,659 --> 00:16:14,563
But a plane far above can still catch the last light of the sun.

310
00:16:14,613 --> 00:16:18,949
Its silver wings flash brightly against a dark sky, like a moving star.

311
00:16:18,999 --> 00:16:24,014
Airline pilots flying across the American West began reporting bright, fast-moving

312
00:16:24,064 --> 00:16:28,585
objects far above them, at altitudes they thought no aircraft could reach.

313
00:16:28,635 --> 00:16:31,550
Many of these reports went to Project Blue Book.

314
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:35,503
According to the CIA's own history, published in 1997, Blue Book

315
00:16:35,553 --> 00:16:40,322
investigators sometimes checked these sightings against the CIA's flight logs.

316
00:16:40,372 --> 00:16:43,534
When the times matched, they knew the UFO was a U-2.

317
00:16:43,584 --> 00:16:45,696
But they could not tell the public.

318
00:16:45,746 --> 00:16:49,093
The history says that over half of all UFO reports from

319
00:16:49,143 --> 00:16:52,861
the late 1950s through the 1960s were accounted for by manned

320
00:16:52,911 --> 00:16:57,988
reconnaissance flights, meaning the U-2 and, later, the even faster A-12 spy plane.

321
00:16:58,038 --> 00:16:59,656
And it says something more.

322
00:16:59,706 --> 00:17:03,177
To hide the existence of these planes, the Air Force made

323
00:17:03,227 --> 00:17:08,119
what the CIA historian called misleading and deceptive statements to the public.

324
00:17:08,169 --> 00:17:11,825
In other words, people who saw something real were told they

325
00:17:11,875 --> 00:17:14,729
had seen something ordinary, or nothing at all.

326
00:17:14,779 --> 00:17:18,620
The CIA's history argues that this was done to protect national

327
00:17:18,670 --> 00:17:20,412
security during the Cold War.

328
00:17:20,462 --> 00:17:22,141
But it also admits the cost.

329
00:17:22,191 --> 00:17:26,033
It fueled later conspiracy theories, and it fed the belief that

330
00:17:26,083 --> 00:17:28,257
the government was hiding the truth.

331
00:17:28,307 --> 00:17:29,430
In a sense, it was.

332
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:31,283
It just was not hiding aliens.

333
00:17:32,333 --> 00:17:35,627
The place where the U-2 was tested has its own legend.

334
00:17:35,677 --> 00:17:38,661
It is a remote stretch of Nevada desert next to a

335
00:17:38,711 --> 00:17:40,580
dry lake bed called Groom Lake.

336
00:17:40,630 --> 00:17:43,491
In the 1950s, the CIA and the Air Force built a

337
00:17:43,541 --> 00:17:47,515
secret airfield there, far from cities and far from curious eyes.

338
00:17:47,565 --> 00:17:50,797
Over the decades it became known by a name taken from

339
00:17:50,847 --> 00:17:52,593
old government maps: Area 51.

340
00:17:52,643 --> 00:17:56,556
For years, the government would not even admit the base existed.

341
00:17:56,606 --> 00:17:58,785
Pilots were told not to fly over it.

342
00:17:58,835 --> 00:17:59,961
Maps left it blank.

343
00:18:00,011 --> 00:18:04,605
And because something secret was clearly happening there, people filled the

344
00:18:04,655 --> 00:18:06,525
silence with their own stories.

345
00:18:06,575 --> 00:18:08,011
Captured flying saucers.

346
00:18:08,061 --> 00:18:08,816
Alien bodies.

347
00:18:08,866 --> 00:18:10,611
Technology from other worlds.

348
00:18:10,661 --> 00:18:14,202
The truth, as far as the released documents show, was more

349
00:18:14,252 --> 00:18:16,432
down to earth, but still remarkable.

350
00:18:16,482 --> 00:18:19,156
Area 51 was a test site for some of the most

351
00:18:19,206 --> 00:18:21,261
advanced aircraft of the Cold War.

352
00:18:21,311 --> 00:18:22,128
First the U-2.

353
00:18:22,178 --> 00:18:25,472
Then the A-12, a sleek black spy plane designed to fly

354
00:18:25,522 --> 00:18:29,558
at more than three times the speed of sound, at the edge of space.

355
00:18:29,608 --> 00:18:32,283
Later, other experimental aircraft followed.

356
00:18:32,333 --> 00:18:36,369
In August 2013, the National Security Archive, a research group at

357
00:18:36,419 --> 00:18:41,137
George Washington University, published a newly released version of the CIA's

358
00:18:41,187 --> 00:18:43,366
internal history of the U-2 program.

359
00:18:43,416 --> 00:18:47,143
An earlier version had blacked out every mention of the site.

360
00:18:47,193 --> 00:18:49,125
In this one, the name was there.

361
00:18:49,175 --> 00:18:52,840
The CIA's own history named Area 51, described what was done

362
00:18:52,890 --> 00:18:55,007
there, and even showed it on a map.

363
00:18:55,057 --> 00:18:59,775
There was no mention of flying saucers, crashed spacecraft or alien visitors.

364
00:18:59,825 --> 00:19:02,871
Just runways, hangars, test pilots and spy planes.

365
00:19:02,921 --> 00:19:06,214
But for many people, that was enough to prove a point.

366
00:19:06,264 --> 00:19:10,053
For more than fifty years, the government had refused to admit

367
00:19:10,103 --> 00:19:11,663
that a real place existed.

368
00:19:11,713 --> 00:19:14,635
It is not hard to see why some still wonder what

369
00:19:14,685 --> 00:19:16,183
else it has not admitted.

370
00:19:17,433 --> 00:19:21,280
On the fourth of October, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik,

371
00:19:21,330 --> 00:19:23,110
the first artificial satellite.

372
00:19:23,160 --> 00:19:26,534
Americans could look up at night and see something made by

373
00:19:26,584 --> 00:19:28,246
their rival crossing the sky.

374
00:19:28,296 --> 00:19:31,552
A month later, on the third of November, a second Soviet

375
00:19:31,602 --> 00:19:34,267
satellite went up, carrying a dog named Laika.

376
00:19:34,317 --> 00:19:38,223
Within days, the United States experienced one of the biggest waves

377
00:19:38,273 --> 00:19:39,994
of UFO reports in its history.

378
00:19:40,044 --> 00:19:43,300
The most famous case happened on the night of the second

379
00:19:43,350 --> 00:19:46,606
of November, near the small town of Levelland, in Texas.

380
00:19:46,656 --> 00:19:51,270
Over a few hours, the sheriff's office received a series of calls from drivers.

381
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:54,930
Several of them described a glowing object, shaped like an egg

382
00:19:54,980 --> 00:19:57,055
or a torpedo, passing over the road.

383
00:19:57,105 --> 00:20:01,010
Some said their car engines and headlights failed as it approached,

384
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:02,663
and came back after it left.

385
00:20:02,713 --> 00:20:05,910
The sheriff himself went out to look, and later said he

386
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:07,977
saw a bright light in the distance.

387
00:20:08,027 --> 00:20:09,807
Project Blue Book investigated.

388
00:20:09,857 --> 00:20:13,172
Its explanation was that the witnesses had seen a form of

389
00:20:13,222 --> 00:20:17,422
ball lightning or an electrical phenomenon connected to the weather, and

390
00:20:17,472 --> 00:20:21,378
that the car problems could be explained by wet electrical systems.

391
00:20:21,428 --> 00:20:24,743
Many people did not accept this, and the case remains one

392
00:20:24,793 --> 00:20:27,163
of the most debated in Blue Book's files.

393
00:20:27,213 --> 00:20:29,406
What is clear is the mood of the time.

394
00:20:29,456 --> 00:20:33,716
The night sky had suddenly become a place where human machines could fly.

395
00:20:33,766 --> 00:20:38,026
Satellites, rockets, re-entering debris and launches would all add to the

396
00:20:38,076 --> 00:20:40,623
flood of reports in the years that followed.

397
00:20:40,673 --> 00:20:44,756
In 1958, the United States launched its first satellite, Explorer One,

398
00:20:44,806 --> 00:20:46,291
and the space race was on.

399
00:20:46,341 --> 00:20:49,183
From then on, the sky would never be empty again.

400
00:20:50,233 --> 00:20:53,608
In 1966, after the swamp gas affair, the Air Force asked

401
00:20:53,658 --> 00:20:58,501
the University of Colorado to carry out an independent scientific study of UFOs.

402
00:20:58,551 --> 00:21:02,354
The project was led by Edward Condon, a respected physicist and

403
00:21:02,404 --> 00:21:05,657
a former director of the National Bureau of Standards.

404
00:21:05,707 --> 00:21:09,877
For about two years, a team of scientists studied cases, photographs,

405
00:21:09,927 --> 00:21:12,079
radar reports, and witness accounts.

406
00:21:12,129 --> 00:21:13,669
Some cases were explained.

407
00:21:13,719 --> 00:21:14,525
Some were not.

408
00:21:14,575 --> 00:21:18,929
The project also had internal disagreements, and some team members later

409
00:21:18,979 --> 00:21:21,620
said its conclusions were decided too early.

410
00:21:21,670 --> 00:21:26,329
The final report, titled Scientific Study of Unidentified Flying Objects, was

411
00:21:26,379 --> 00:21:27,858
released in January 1969.

412
00:21:27,908 --> 00:21:31,589
It was long, close to a thousand pages in its published form.

413
00:21:31,639 --> 00:21:33,485
Condon's own summary was blunt.

414
00:21:33,535 --> 00:21:36,971
He concluded that nothing had come from the study of UFOs

415
00:21:37,021 --> 00:21:41,497
over the previous twenty-one years that had added to scientific knowledge,

416
00:21:41,547 --> 00:21:45,595
and that further extensive study probably could not be justified in

417
00:21:45,645 --> 00:21:48,836
the expectation that science would be advanced by it.

418
00:21:48,886 --> 00:21:52,934
A committee of the National Academy of Sciences reviewed the report

419
00:21:52,984 --> 00:21:54,708
and agreed with its approach.

420
00:21:54,758 --> 00:21:57,399
That gave the Air Force the cover it needed.

421
00:21:57,449 --> 00:22:01,191
On the seventeenth of December, 1969, the Secretary of the Air

422
00:22:01,241 --> 00:22:04,249
Force announced that Project Blue Book was closed.

423
00:22:04,299 --> 00:22:07,551
In its final count, the Air Force said it had received

424
00:22:07,601 --> 00:22:11,894
twelve thousand six hundred and eighteen reports between 1947 and 1969.

425
00:22:11,944 --> 00:22:15,196
Of those, seven hundred and one remained unidentified.

426
00:22:15,246 --> 00:22:17,459
The Air Force gave three conclusions.

427
00:22:17,509 --> 00:22:21,802
No UFO it had investigated had ever been a threat to national security.

428
00:22:21,852 --> 00:22:26,878
There was no evidence that the unidentified sightings represented technology beyond

429
00:22:26,928 --> 00:22:28,285
the science of the day.

430
00:22:28,335 --> 00:22:32,811
And there was no evidence that any of them were extraterrestrial vehicles.

431
00:22:32,861 --> 00:22:34,584
Officially, that was the end.

432
00:22:34,634 --> 00:22:38,132
Seven hundred and one cases, filed away without an answer.

433
00:22:38,182 --> 00:22:42,229
For the next fifty years, the government's public position was that

434
00:22:42,279 --> 00:22:44,553
it was no longer studying the subject.

435
00:22:44,603 --> 00:22:47,183
As we will see, that was not entirely true.

436
00:22:48,267 --> 00:22:51,303
In the 1970s, interest in UFOs did not disappear.

437
00:22:51,353 --> 00:22:55,398
It moved to private research groups, to books, and to television.

438
00:22:55,448 --> 00:22:57,225
And it moved into the courts.

439
00:22:57,275 --> 00:23:00,626
A UFO group used the new Freedom of Information Act to

440
00:23:00,676 --> 00:23:02,390
sue the CIA for its records.

441
00:23:02,440 --> 00:23:06,737
In 1978 and 1979, the agency released hundreds of pages of documents.

442
00:23:06,787 --> 00:23:10,957
Most showed limited and scattered interest in the subject after the

443
00:23:11,007 --> 00:23:13,477
1950s, but the release proved one thing.

444
00:23:13,527 --> 00:23:16,753
The CIA had been involved more than it had admitted.

445
00:23:16,803 --> 00:23:18,202
Then Roswell came back.

446
00:23:18,252 --> 00:23:22,485
After Jesse Marcel's interviews and a best-selling book in 1980, the

447
00:23:22,535 --> 00:23:27,966
story grew into a full mystery, with claims of crashed spacecraft and recovered bodies.

448
00:23:28,016 --> 00:23:31,997
In 1993, a congressman from New Mexico, Steven Schiff, asked the

449
00:23:32,047 --> 00:23:35,777
government's investigators to find out what records existed.

450
00:23:35,827 --> 00:23:38,108
Two answers came out of that request.

451
00:23:38,158 --> 00:23:43,210
First, the General Accounting Office, the investigative arm of Congress, searched

452
00:23:43,260 --> 00:23:47,683
for records and reported that some of the Roswell base's administrative

453
00:23:47,733 --> 00:23:52,092
records and outgoing messages from those years had been destroyed long

454
00:23:52,142 --> 00:23:55,809
before, and that the records did not show who had destroyed

455
00:23:55,859 --> 00:23:58,077
them, when, or under what authority.

456
00:23:58,127 --> 00:24:00,659
Second, the Air Force did its own search.

457
00:24:00,709 --> 00:24:04,502
In 1994, it reported that the Roswell debris most likely came

458
00:24:04,552 --> 00:24:07,526
from a once secret program called Project Mogul.

459
00:24:07,576 --> 00:24:12,439
Mogul used long trains of balloons, carrying microphones and radar reflectors,

460
00:24:12,489 --> 00:24:16,912
to listen high in the atmosphere for the sound of Soviet nuclear tests.

461
00:24:16,962 --> 00:24:18,613
In 1947, it was classified.

462
00:24:18,663 --> 00:24:22,266
The officers at Roswell would not have known about it, and

463
00:24:22,316 --> 00:24:26,298
the weather balloon story was a simple explanation for what was,

464
00:24:26,348 --> 00:24:27,936
in fact, a secret project.

465
00:24:27,986 --> 00:24:30,519
That still left the stories about bodies.

466
00:24:30,569 --> 00:24:34,361
In 1997, on the fiftieth anniversary of the incident, the Air

467
00:24:34,411 --> 00:24:38,834
Force released a second report, titled The Roswell Report: Case Closed.

468
00:24:38,884 --> 00:24:43,495
It argued that some witnesses may have been remembering a different event.

469
00:24:43,545 --> 00:24:47,464
In the 1950s, the Air Force had dropped life-sized dummies from

470
00:24:47,514 --> 00:24:50,803
high-altitude balloons to test parachutes for pilots.

471
00:24:50,853 --> 00:24:54,897
Over time, the report suggested, memories of these dummies and of

472
00:24:54,947 --> 00:24:58,740
real aircraft accidents may have blended with the 1947 story.

473
00:24:58,790 --> 00:25:03,716
Many researchers rejected this, because the dummy tests began years after 1947.

474
00:25:03,766 --> 00:25:08,630
The Air Force admitted the timeline problem, but it maintained its conclusion.

475
00:25:08,680 --> 00:25:12,598
Whatever you think of that explanation, the paper trail shows a

476
00:25:12,648 --> 00:25:14,362
pattern we have seen before.

477
00:25:14,412 --> 00:25:19,717
A secret program, an incomplete public answer, and decades of distrust that followed.

478
00:25:20,833 --> 00:25:24,251
In 1997, the same year as the second Roswell report, the

479
00:25:24,301 --> 00:25:28,275
CIA published an article in its journal, Studies in Intelligence.

480
00:25:28,325 --> 00:25:32,176
It was written by Gerald Haines, the agency's historian, and it

481
00:25:32,226 --> 00:25:35,705
was titled CIA's Role in the Study of UFOs, 1947 to 1990.

482
00:25:35,755 --> 00:25:39,234
It is one of the most remarkable documents in this story,

483
00:25:39,284 --> 00:25:42,826
because it is an official history written from the inside.

484
00:25:42,876 --> 00:25:45,859
It describes the creation of the Robertson Panel.

485
00:25:45,909 --> 00:25:49,822
It describes the agency's worry about the Soviet Union using UFO

486
00:25:49,872 --> 00:25:51,246
reports to cause panic.

487
00:25:51,296 --> 00:25:54,837
And it openly states that U-2 and A-12 flights were behind

488
00:25:54,887 --> 00:25:58,181
many of the sightings of the 1950s and 1960s, and that

489
00:25:58,231 --> 00:26:01,153
the Air Force misled the public to protect them.

490
00:26:01,203 --> 00:26:04,930
The history also tries to explain why, after the early 1950s,

491
00:26:04,980 --> 00:26:07,654
the CIA paid only limited attention to UFOs.

492
00:26:07,704 --> 00:26:10,997
In its view, there was simply no evidence of a threat,

493
00:26:11,047 --> 00:26:13,474
and the agency had more urgent concerns.

494
00:26:13,524 --> 00:26:17,127
Haines also drew a lesson that reads almost like a warning.

495
00:26:17,177 --> 00:26:21,028
He noted that the misleading statements made to protect the spy

496
00:26:21,078 --> 00:26:26,353
planes helped fuel the conspiracy theories and the cover-up accusations that followed.

497
00:26:26,403 --> 00:26:31,182
When an organization hides some things, people assume it is hiding everything.

498
00:26:31,232 --> 00:26:34,278
That sentence was written almost thirty years ago.

499
00:26:34,328 --> 00:26:37,683
The next chapter of the UFO files would prove it right.

500
00:26:38,767 --> 00:26:42,000
In the same year that the CIA published its history, one

501
00:26:42,050 --> 00:26:45,812
of the most famous mass sightings in American history took place.

502
00:26:45,862 --> 00:26:49,388
On the evening of the thirteenth of March, 1997, thousands of

503
00:26:49,438 --> 00:26:52,731
people across Arizona reported strange lights in the sky.

504
00:26:52,781 --> 00:26:55,956
Many in and around the city of Phoenix described a huge

505
00:26:56,006 --> 00:26:59,767
V-shaped formation of lights moving slowly and silently overhead.

506
00:26:59,817 --> 00:27:03,226
Later that night, a row of bright orange lights appeared to

507
00:27:03,276 --> 00:27:07,038
hang in the sky south of the city, and was filmed by many people.

508
00:27:07,088 --> 00:27:11,377
The military's explanation came later, and it focused on the second event.

509
00:27:11,427 --> 00:27:15,423
It said the hovering lights were flares, dropped by military aircraft

510
00:27:15,473 --> 00:27:19,351
taking part in a training exercise at a range southwest of Phoenix.

511
00:27:19,401 --> 00:27:23,045
As the flares slowly fell on their parachutes, the mountains in

512
00:27:23,095 --> 00:27:25,273
between eventually hid them from view.

513
00:27:25,323 --> 00:27:30,257
Analysts who studied the videos have supported this explanation for the later lights.

514
00:27:30,307 --> 00:27:33,072
The earlier V-shaped formation is more disputed.

515
00:27:33,122 --> 00:27:36,414
Skeptics have argued it was a group of aircraft flying in

516
00:27:36,464 --> 00:27:39,522
formation, their lights blurred together by distance.

517
00:27:39,572 --> 00:27:43,392
Some witnesses, including people who saw it at close range, reject

518
00:27:43,442 --> 00:27:46,382
that and insist they saw one enormous solid object.

519
00:27:46,432 --> 00:27:47,438
Then came a twist.

520
00:27:47,488 --> 00:27:51,601
Arizona's governor at the time, Fife Symington, held a press conference

521
00:27:51,651 --> 00:27:54,767
in which an aide appeared in an alien costume, to make

522
00:27:54,817 --> 00:27:55,764
fun of the panic.

523
00:27:55,814 --> 00:27:59,692
Years later, Symington said that he himself had seen something that

524
00:27:59,742 --> 00:28:03,210
night he could not explain, and that he had regretted making

525
00:28:03,260 --> 00:28:03,973
a joke of it.

526
00:28:04,023 --> 00:28:07,843
The Phoenix Lights show how even a well-documented event can split

527
00:28:07,893 --> 00:28:11,830
into two stories, one with an official explanation that many accept,

528
00:28:11,880 --> 00:28:14,117
and one that many witnesses never will.

529
00:28:15,233 --> 00:28:19,064
On the sixteenth of December, 2017, the New York Times published

530
00:28:19,114 --> 00:28:21,368
a story that changed the conversation.

531
00:28:21,418 --> 00:28:25,188
It revealed that the Pentagon had run a little-known program to

532
00:28:25,238 --> 00:28:28,523
study unidentified aerial phenomena, from 2007 to 2012.

533
00:28:28,573 --> 00:28:32,585
It was called the Advanced Aerospace Threat Identification Program.

534
00:28:32,635 --> 00:28:36,345
Its existence had been pushed for by several senators, and the

535
00:28:36,395 --> 00:28:40,589
Times reported that it had received around twenty-two million dollars.

536
00:28:40,639 --> 00:28:42,226
The story came with videos.

537
00:28:42,276 --> 00:28:46,592
Footage from the targeting cameras of Navy fighter jets, showing objects

538
00:28:46,642 --> 00:28:48,714
that the pilots could not identify.

539
00:28:48,764 --> 00:28:52,352
The first video was recorded in November 2004, off the coast

540
00:28:52,402 --> 00:28:57,749
of Southern California, during training exercises around the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz.

541
00:28:57,799 --> 00:29:01,205
According to the pilots, radar on a Navy cruiser had been

542
00:29:01,255 --> 00:29:03,266
tracking unusual objects for days.

543
00:29:03,316 --> 00:29:05,571
Fighter jets were sent to investigate.

544
00:29:05,621 --> 00:29:09,087
One of the pilots later described a white object, shaped a

545
00:29:09,137 --> 00:29:12,301
little like a large Tic Tac candy, that moved in ways

546
00:29:12,351 --> 00:29:13,817
he had never seen before.

547
00:29:13,867 --> 00:29:16,909
A second crew recorded an object on infrared video.

548
00:29:16,959 --> 00:29:18,850
That clip became known as FLIR1.

549
00:29:18,900 --> 00:29:22,609
Two more videos came from 2015, recorded by pilots flying from

550
00:29:22,659 --> 00:29:25,883
the carrier USS Theodore Roosevelt off the East Coast.

551
00:29:25,933 --> 00:29:29,218
In one, known as Gimbal, an object appears to rotate as

552
00:29:29,268 --> 00:29:30,795
it moves against the wind.

553
00:29:30,845 --> 00:29:33,948
In the other, known as Go Fast, a small object seems

554
00:29:33,998 --> 00:29:35,585
to race low over the ocean.

555
00:29:35,635 --> 00:29:38,495
On the audio, you can hear the pilots' surprise.

556
00:29:38,545 --> 00:29:42,497
For a while, the government did not officially confirm the videos.

557
00:29:42,547 --> 00:29:47,226
Then, in 2019, the Navy said that they did show unidentified aerial phenomena.

558
00:29:47,276 --> 00:29:51,289
And on the twenty-seventh of April, 2020, the Department of Defense

559
00:29:51,339 --> 00:29:53,108
officially released all three.

560
00:29:53,158 --> 00:29:56,382
It said it was doing so to clear up any misconceptions

561
00:29:56,432 --> 00:29:58,505
about whether the footage was real.

562
00:29:58,555 --> 00:30:01,839
It is important to be precise about what the Navy said.

563
00:30:01,889 --> 00:30:03,780
Unidentified means exactly that.

564
00:30:03,830 --> 00:30:04,386
Not alien.

565
00:30:04,436 --> 00:30:05,114
Not foreign.

566
00:30:05,164 --> 00:30:05,599
Unknown.

567
00:30:05,649 --> 00:30:10,571
Since then, analysts outside the government have offered ordinary explanations for

568
00:30:10,621 --> 00:30:13,663
some of what is seen in these clips, such as camera

569
00:30:13,713 --> 00:30:17,908
effects, distant aircraft, and the way infrared cameras track objects.

570
00:30:17,958 --> 00:30:22,395
Others argue those explanations do not fit everything the pilots reported.

571
00:30:22,445 --> 00:30:23,728
That debate continues.

572
00:30:23,778 --> 00:30:27,185
But for the first time in fifty years, the government was

573
00:30:27,235 --> 00:30:30,883
publicly saying it did not know what its own pilots had seen.

574
00:30:32,033 --> 00:30:34,477
Congress responded by asking for answers.

575
00:30:34,527 --> 00:30:38,432
In June 2021, the Office of the Director of National Intelligence

576
00:30:38,482 --> 00:30:44,028
released a short public report called Preliminary Assessment: Unidentified Aerial Phenomena.

577
00:30:44,078 --> 00:30:47,374
It looked at one hundred and forty-four reports from U.

578
00:30:47,424 --> 00:30:47,495
S.

579
00:30:47,545 --> 00:30:52,545
government sources, mostly from military personnel, recorded between 2004 and 2021.

580
00:30:52,595 --> 00:30:56,316
Of those, exactly one could be explained with high confidence.

581
00:30:56,366 --> 00:30:58,384
It was a large, deflating balloon.

582
00:30:58,434 --> 00:31:05,258
Eighteen incidents, described in twenty-one reports, appeared to show unusual movement or flight characteristics.

583
00:31:05,308 --> 00:31:08,483
The report was careful to say that some of this might

584
00:31:08,533 --> 00:31:12,132
be caused by sensor errors or by the way observers perceived

585
00:31:12,182 --> 00:31:15,113
what they saw, and that more analysis was needed.

586
00:31:15,163 --> 00:31:18,763
The report proposed five possible categories of explanation.

587
00:31:18,813 --> 00:31:22,048
Airborne clutter, such as birds, balloons, and drones.

588
00:31:22,098 --> 00:31:23,751
Natural atmospheric effects.

589
00:31:23,801 --> 00:31:28,253
Secret developmental programs of the United States government or industry.

590
00:31:28,303 --> 00:31:32,025
Systems built by foreign adversaries, such as China or Russia.

591
00:31:32,075 --> 00:31:34,579
And a fifth category, simply called other.

592
00:31:34,629 --> 00:31:39,568
Many headlines focused on the fact that the government had not ruled out anything.

593
00:31:39,618 --> 00:31:42,670
But the real message of the report was more modest.

594
00:31:42,720 --> 00:31:47,658
The military had not been collecting data properly, and it needed a better system.

595
00:31:47,708 --> 00:31:49,483
That system would soon arrive.

596
00:31:50,667 --> 00:31:54,992
Then, in early 2023, the question of unidentified objects suddenly became

597
00:31:55,042 --> 00:31:58,048
front-page news again, for a very different reason.

598
00:31:58,098 --> 00:32:01,884
In late January, a large white balloon was spotted drifting high

599
00:32:01,934 --> 00:32:03,262
over the United States.

600
00:32:03,312 --> 00:32:06,738
The government said it was a Chinese surveillance balloon.

601
00:32:06,788 --> 00:32:11,532
China said it was a civilian weather research airship that had blown off course.

602
00:32:11,582 --> 00:32:15,308
For days, Americans watched it cross the country, until, on the

603
00:32:15,358 --> 00:32:18,604
fourth of February, a US Air Force F-22 fighter shot it

604
00:32:18,654 --> 00:32:20,822
down off the coast of South Carolina.

605
00:32:20,872 --> 00:32:23,518
The Navy recovered its debris from the ocean.

606
00:32:23,568 --> 00:32:27,654
Within the next week, American fighters shot down three more objects,

607
00:32:27,704 --> 00:32:32,208
one over Alaska, one over Canada's Yukon Territory, and one over Lake Huron.

608
00:32:32,258 --> 00:32:35,504
For a few days, the government admitted it did not know

609
00:32:35,554 --> 00:32:36,883
exactly what they were.

610
00:32:36,933 --> 00:32:41,498
Officials later said they were most likely small, harmless balloons, possibly

611
00:32:41,548 --> 00:32:45,992
used by hobbyists or companies, and nothing like the large Chinese balloon.

612
00:32:46,042 --> 00:32:48,449
The episode revealed something important.

613
00:32:48,499 --> 00:32:52,225
After the military adjusted its radar systems to look for slow,

614
00:32:52,275 --> 00:32:55,821
small objects, it suddenly started seeing many more of them.

615
00:32:55,871 --> 00:32:58,877
The sky, it turned out, was full of things that had

616
00:32:58,927 --> 00:33:02,233
always been there, but that nobody had been looking for.

617
00:33:02,283 --> 00:33:05,589
It was the Washington radar flap of 1952 all over again.

618
00:33:05,639 --> 00:33:08,286
Better sensors, more reports, more confusion.

619
00:33:08,336 --> 00:33:11,702
And once again, balloons were at the center of the story,

620
00:33:11,752 --> 00:33:14,759
just as they had been at Roswell and in the Mantell

621
00:33:14,809 --> 00:33:16,617
case seventy-five years before.

622
00:33:17,700 --> 00:33:18,911
NASA also stepped in.

623
00:33:18,961 --> 00:33:22,574
In 2022, the space agency formed an independent study team of

624
00:33:22,624 --> 00:33:26,717
scientists and experts to look at how unidentified phenomena could be

625
00:33:26,767 --> 00:33:28,998
studied using real scientific methods.

626
00:33:29,048 --> 00:33:33,022
On the fourteenth of September, 2023, the team released its report.

627
00:33:33,072 --> 00:33:37,465
It found no evidence that these phenomena have an extraterrestrial origin.

628
00:33:37,515 --> 00:33:40,587
But it also did not claim to know what they all are.

629
00:33:40,637 --> 00:33:45,091
NASA's administrator, Bill Nelson, said it plainly at the press conference:

630
00:33:45,141 --> 00:33:47,072
we don't know what these UAP are.

631
00:33:47,122 --> 00:33:49,414
The team's main message was about data.

632
00:33:49,464 --> 00:33:53,317
Most reports, it said, come from sensors that were never designed

633
00:33:53,367 --> 00:33:56,619
for this kind of work, from cameras and radars built to

634
00:33:56,669 --> 00:34:00,162
track aircraft and targets, not to measure unknown objects.

635
00:34:00,212 --> 00:34:05,086
To get real answers, scientists need multiple well-calibrated sensors watching the

636
00:34:05,136 --> 00:34:08,388
same event, and they need the data to be shared openly.

637
00:34:08,438 --> 00:34:12,291
NASA appointed a director of UAP research, a scientist named Mark

638
00:34:12,341 --> 00:34:16,674
McInerney, and committed to using its satellites and instruments to help.

639
00:34:16,724 --> 00:34:20,157
It also asked the public to approach the subject with less

640
00:34:20,207 --> 00:34:24,660
fear and less stigma, so that pilots and others would report what they see.

641
00:34:24,710 --> 00:34:26,161
It was a striking moment.

642
00:34:26,211 --> 00:34:30,905
Seventy-five years after Kenneth Arnold's flight, the official tone had changed

643
00:34:30,955 --> 00:34:32,526
from ridicule to curiosity.

644
00:34:32,576 --> 00:34:36,189
The conclusion was the same, no evidence of visitors, but the

645
00:34:36,239 --> 00:34:37,750
door was no longer closed.

646
00:34:38,802 --> 00:34:42,483
In 2022, the Department of Defense created a new office called

647
00:34:42,533 --> 00:34:45,853
the All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office, known as AARO.

648
00:34:45,903 --> 00:34:50,065
Its job was to collect and investigate reports of unidentified objects

649
00:34:50,115 --> 00:34:52,472
in the air, in space, and under the sea.

650
00:34:52,522 --> 00:34:55,902
For the first time since Blue Book, there was a permanent

651
00:34:55,952 --> 00:34:57,827
official office for the subject.

652
00:34:57,877 --> 00:34:59,753
The pressure on it grew quickly.

653
00:34:59,803 --> 00:35:03,965
In July 2023, a former Air Force intelligence officer testified before

654
00:35:04,015 --> 00:35:05,409
a committee of Congress.

655
00:35:05,459 --> 00:35:08,899
He said he had been told about a secret government program

656
00:35:08,949 --> 00:35:14,014
that had recovered craft of non-human origin and was trying to reverse engineer them.

657
00:35:14,064 --> 00:35:17,203
He said he had not seen the evidence himself, but had

658
00:35:17,253 --> 00:35:18,467
heard it from others.

659
00:35:18,517 --> 00:35:21,114
The hearing made headlines around the world.

660
00:35:21,164 --> 00:35:24,364
AARO was asked to look into these claims, and into the

661
00:35:24,414 --> 00:35:27,372
whole history of government involvement with UFOs.

662
00:35:27,422 --> 00:35:30,863
On the eighth of March, 2024, it released the first volume

663
00:35:30,913 --> 00:35:32,788
of its Historical Record Report.

664
00:35:32,838 --> 00:35:37,121
It covered official government investigations from 1945 to October 2023.

665
00:35:37,171 --> 00:35:38,805
Its conclusions were direct.

666
00:35:38,855 --> 00:35:43,559
AARO found no evidence that any government investigation, academic research, or

667
00:35:43,609 --> 00:35:49,216
official review panel had confirmed that any sighting represented extraterrestrial technology.

668
00:35:49,266 --> 00:35:54,390
It found no verifiable evidence of any secret program to reverse engineer alien craft.

669
00:35:54,440 --> 00:35:58,061
It said that many reports could be traced to ordinary objects

670
00:35:58,111 --> 00:36:01,551
and to secret but very human programs, such as spy planes,

671
00:36:01,601 --> 00:36:04,440
satellites, rockets, and high-altitude balloons.

672
00:36:04,490 --> 00:36:08,592
The report also offered an explanation for why these stories persist.

673
00:36:08,642 --> 00:36:12,623
Real secret programs, rumors about them, and the natural human wish

674
00:36:12,673 --> 00:36:16,414
for a bigger answer had combined, over decades, into a powerful

675
00:36:16,464 --> 00:36:18,761
belief that the truth was being hidden.

676
00:36:18,811 --> 00:36:22,191
Critics said the report was itself a cover-up, or that it

677
00:36:22,241 --> 00:36:24,357
did not address the strongest cases.

678
00:36:24,407 --> 00:36:28,389
Supporters said it was the most thorough official review ever made.

679
00:36:28,439 --> 00:36:32,360
And so, more than seventy years after Kenneth Arnold's flight, the

680
00:36:32,410 --> 00:36:36,452
debate continues, with new reports, new hearings, and new questions.

681
00:36:37,635 --> 00:36:39,575
So what do the files actually show?

682
00:36:39,625 --> 00:36:43,499
They show that from the very beginning, the government's main concern

683
00:36:43,549 --> 00:36:45,546
was not visitors from another world.

684
00:36:45,596 --> 00:36:48,559
It was the Soviet Union, the safety of the skies, and

685
00:36:48,609 --> 00:36:51,118
the danger of confusion during a real attack.

686
00:36:51,168 --> 00:36:53,733
They show that the government did hide things.

687
00:36:53,783 --> 00:36:55,780
A secret balloon program at Roswell.

688
00:36:55,830 --> 00:36:58,112
Spy planes behind thousands of sightings.

689
00:36:58,162 --> 00:37:02,319
A scientific panel that recommended taking the mystery out of the subject.

690
00:37:02,369 --> 00:37:05,447
Statements to the public that were, in the words of the

691
00:37:05,497 --> 00:37:08,062
CIA's own historian, misleading and deceptive.

692
00:37:08,112 --> 00:37:11,644
They show that every official study, from Project Sign to AARO,

693
00:37:11,694 --> 00:37:16,875
has reached the same final conclusion: no confirmed evidence of extraterrestrial technology.

694
00:37:16,925 --> 00:37:21,197
And they show something else, which may be the most important lesson of all.

695
00:37:21,247 --> 00:37:24,551
A small number of cases, from Blue Book's seven hundred and

696
00:37:24,601 --> 00:37:29,441
one unidentified files to the Navy videos of today, remain without a confirmed answer.

697
00:37:29,491 --> 00:37:31,318
Unidentified does not mean alien.

698
00:37:31,368 --> 00:37:33,308
But it does not mean solved either.

699
00:37:33,358 --> 00:37:36,208
The deepest story in the UFO files may not be about

700
00:37:36,258 --> 00:37:37,061
the sky at all.

701
00:37:37,111 --> 00:37:38,084
It is about trust.

702
00:37:38,134 --> 00:37:42,007
Each time officials hid a secret program behind a simple explanation,

703
00:37:42,057 --> 00:37:46,385
they bought a little security, and they spent a little of the public's trust.

704
00:37:46,435 --> 00:37:49,285
Seventy years later, that bill is still being paid.

705
00:37:50,335 --> 00:37:53,568
Thank you for watching this film from The Declassified Desk.

706
00:37:53,618 --> 00:37:56,796
Every document we used is listed in the description, so you

707
00:37:56,846 --> 00:37:58,055
can read them yourself.

708
00:37:58,105 --> 00:37:59,587
Now I want to hear from you.

709
00:37:59,637 --> 00:38:02,377
After everything in these files, what do you think?

710
00:38:02,427 --> 00:38:06,207
Were all these sightings secret aircraft, balloons and mistakes, or is

711
00:38:06,257 --> 00:38:08,778
there something the files still do not explain?

712
00:38:08,828 --> 00:38:10,091
Tell me in the comments.

713
00:38:10,141 --> 00:38:12,991
And if you want to see more real documents behind the

714
00:38:13,041 --> 00:38:15,399
legends, subscribe to The Declassified Desk.

715
00:38:15,449 --> 00:38:16,985
We open a new file every day.

